Kubernetes Custom Resources

## Overview

Kubernetes ships with a set of native, out-of-the-box resources:

- `Deployment`
- `Service`
- `Pod`
- `ConfigMap`
- `Secret`
- `Ingress`

When a required capability is **not** covered by these native resources, Kubernetes allows me to **extend its API** by introducing new resource types.

---

## Why Extend the Kubernetes API?

Real-world tools that extend Kubernetes via custom resources:

| Tool | Capability Added |
|------|-----------------|
| **Istio** | Service mesh, Mutual TLS, east-west traffic |
| **Argo CD** | GitOps / continuous delivery |
| **Flux** | GitOps |
| **Keycloak** | Identity & access management, OAuth, OIDC |
| **Kyverno** | Policy engine / security |
| **kube-bench** | CIS security benchmarking |
| **Prometheus** | Metrics & monitoring |
| **Crossplane** | Infrastructure as code |

> Kubernetes cannot bake logic for every third-party tool into its control plane — that's why the extension mechanism exists.

---

## The Three Core Concepts

```
CRD  →  CR  →  Custom Controller
```

| Concept | Short Form | Who Deploys It | Purpose |
|---------|-----------|----------------|---------|
| Custom Resource Definition | CRD | DevOps Engineer | Defines + registers a new API type in Kubernetes |
| Custom Resource | CR | User / DevOps Engineer | An instance of that new API type |
| Custom Controller | — | DevOps Engineer | Watches CRs and acts on them |

---

## Custom Resource Definition (CRD)

- A **YAML file** submitted to Kubernetes that introduces a new API type
- Defines all valid fields a user can include in a Custom Resource
- Acts as the **schema/template** for the new resource — just like Kubernetes has a built-in schema for `Deployment`
- Kubernetes validates every CR submission against its corresponding CRD
- If I add an unknown field, Kubernetes throws: `field XYZ is not known`

### Native Resource vs Custom Resource (comparison)

| | Native Resource | Custom Resource |
|-|----------------|-----------------|
| YAML submitted by user | `deployment.yaml` | `virtual-service.yaml` |
| Validated against | Built-in resource definition | CRD |
| Processed by | Built-in controller | Custom controller |

### Example CRD structure (conceptual)

```yaml
apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: virtualservices.networking.istio.io
spec:
  group: networking.istio.io
  versions:
- name: v1alpha3
      served: true
      storage: true
  scope: Namespaced
  names:
    plural: virtualservices
    singular: virtualservice
    kind: VirtualService
```

---

## Custom Resource (CR)

- An **instance** of a type defined by a CRD
- Created by end users or DevOps engineers
- Validated against the CRD before being accepted by the API server

### Example CR (Istio VirtualService)

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1alpha3
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: my-virtual-service
  namespace: my-namespace
spec:
  hosts:
- my-service
  http:
- route:
- destination:
            host: my-service
            port:
              number: 8080
```

> ⚠️ Deploying a CR without a running Custom Controller = nothing happens. (Same as deploying an `Ingress` without an Ingress controller.)

---

## Custom Controller

- A program that **watches** for CR events (create / update / delete) and performs the desired actions
- Must be deployed to the cluster **before** (or alongside) any CRs it manages
- Analogous to the native `DeploymentController` inside `kube-controller-manager`

### How it works internally (high level)

```
User creates CR
       │
       ▼
API Server accepts (validated against CRD)
       │
       ▼
Custom Controller (watching via Informer/Watcher)
       │
       ▼
Event added to Worker Queue
       │
       ▼
Controller processes event → performs action on cluster
```

---

## End-to-End Flow

```
[DevOps Engineer]                [User]
       │                            │
1. Deploy CRD              3. Create CR (e.g. VirtualService)
2. Deploy Custom                  │
     Controller              Validated against CRD
                                    │
                             Custom Controller watches CR
                                    │
                             Performs required action
                             (e.g. configure service mesh)
```

---

## Writing a Custom Controller

- Preferred language: **Go (Golang)**
- Kubernetes itself is written in Go
- Strong community support
- Excellent concurrency primitives

- Core Go package: [`client-go`](https://github.com/kubernetes/client-go)
- Allows interaction with the Kubernetes API server programmatically

### Key internal components

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Informer / Watcher** | Listens for resource events (create/update/delete) |
| **Reflector** | Syncs state from API server into local cache |
| **Worker Queue** | Queues events for processing |
| **Reconciler** | Processes each queued object and drives actual state → desired state |

### Popular frameworks

- [`controller-runtime`](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/controller-runtime) — official Kubernetes Go framework for building controllers
- [`operator-sdk`](https://sdk.operatorframework.io/) — higher-level framework for building Operators (controllers + more)

> **Operator** = Controller + additional lifecycle management logic (install, upgrade, backup, etc.)

### Learning resource

- [kubernetes/sample-controller](https://github.com/kubernetes/sample-controller) — official minimal example

---

## Deploying with Helm (Istio Example)

```bash
# 1. Add the Istio Helm repo
helm repo add istio https://istio-release.storage.googleapis.com/charts
helm repo update

# 2. Create the istio-system namespace
kubectl create namespace istio-system

# 3. Install Istio base (deploys CRDs)
helm install istio-base istio/base -n istio-system

# 4. Install Istiod (deploys the custom controller)
helm install istiod istio/istiod -n istio-system --wait
```

```bash
# Verify CRDs were registered
kubectl get crd | grep istio
```

Expected output includes entries like:
```
virtualservices.networking.istio.io
destinationrules.networking.istio.io
gateways.networking.istio.io
...
```

> The same Helm-based install pattern applies to **Argo CD**, **Prometheus**, **Crossplane**, and most CNCF projects.

---

## DevOps Engineer Responsibilities

### Installation
- [ ] Deploy the **CRD** (registers new API types)
- [ ] Deploy the **Custom Controller** (processes CR events)
- [ ] Guide users in creating **Custom Resources**

### Day-to-day operations
- Understand the tool's concepts deeply (e.g., what is a VirtualService in Istio?)
- Debug issues when CRs are not working as expected:

```bash
# Describe a custom resource
kubectl describe virtualservice <name> -n <namespace>

# Check controller logs
kubectl logs -n istio-system deploy/istiod

# List all custom resources of a type
kubectl get virtualservices -A
```
